<GuideLayout 
  title="The Science of Compression: Why It's More Than Just Tight Fabric" 
  type="TECH WEAR"
  description="Understand the physiological benefits of graduated compression. From muscle recovery to increased blood flow."
>
  <h2>Engineered for Recovery</h2>
  <p>Compression wear is designed to apply specific pressure to your limbs. It's a staple in both high-performance sports and post-workout recovery.</p>
  
  <h3>Graduated Compression</h3>
  <p>This technique applies the most pressure at the ankles/wrists and decreases as it moves toward the heart. This promotes better venous return and reduces muscle vibration.</p>

  <h3>Material Composition</h3>
  <p>High compression requires a high Spandex/Elastane content (typically 15-25%). The weave must be tight enough to provide support without restricting movement.</p>

  <h3>Consumer Benefits</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Reduced muscle soreness (DOMS)</li>
    <li>Improved athletic performance through better circulation</li>
    <li>A "sculpted" aesthetic that many customers prefer</li>
  </ul>
</GuideLayout>
